Consider the vector $\mathbf{a}$ shown in the picture below. The vector $2\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the same direction as $\mathbf{a}$ and a length (magnitude) that is twice the length of $\mathbf{a}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\begin{vmatrix}2\mathbf{a} &= 2 \cdot & \mathbf{a}\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

![](images/01/01c393b2df3d39e4f62faca475a26ea835e1ab88266628509d58e21edd36c79b.png)

On the other hand, $-1.5\mathbf{a}$ is the vector that has the opposite direction to $\mathbf{a}$ and its magnitude is $1.5$ times the magnitude of $\mathbf{a}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\begin{vmatrix}-1.5\mathbf{a} &= 1.5 \cdot & \mathbf{a}\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

![](images/73/73ea353af74ab9518ea7a963985ad26ad36723c7e3a335391628f1eba208139e.png)

Notice that $2\mathbf{a}$ and $-1.5\mathbf{a}$ are both parallel to $\mathbf a$, and are parallel to each other.
